**Abstract.** Formative walkthrough of the Kinnect prototype (AT-01 Build 1) with two peers from another group, in the course lab session of 13 October 2026. No answers were written down during the session, so the findings below are the verbal feedback the group recorded afterwards. They bear mostly on how quickly the prototype gets to an introduction (UC02-F3-CL1), on the choice of robot, and on how the demo was presented. A repeat walkthrough with recorded answers is planned, with the step table below as its protocol.

= Evaluation Report [ER-04] =

== Shared fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|ER-04
|Name|Peer walkthrough of the Kinnect prototype, 13 October 2026
|Status|In Progress (one session held; repeat planned)
|Mode|Formative
|Artifact used|[[AT-01 Kinnect social connection-maker>>doc:3 Evaluation.a Artifacts.a1 Kinnect.WebHome]], Build 1 (##sce-poc##), as deployed on 13 October 2026. This was an earlier state of Build 1: the concept was still "Walking with purpose", so a session centred on Pepper inviting a resident on a walk and asking whether another resident should join. The concept was narrowed to introductions three days later. On the day, the deployment was updated so that any of the persona cards could be used.
|Evaluation Method(s) applied|(((
Ad hoc, not one of EM-01 to EM-04. Two peers from another group used the prototype in the role of residents, with a group member facilitating, in the lab session set aside for walkthroughs. The four walkthrough questions used in ER-01 were the intended frame, but they were not applied step by step. The session addressed Claims, since the peers judged whether the interaction would work for a resident; it did not verify any Premise.
)))
|Ethics|No ethics review was required. The peers were students taking part in the course's lab session, playing personas from the persona cards. No personal data were recorded, and no resident or other person outside the course took part.
|Introduction|(((
Aimed at the Claims on asking, matching and introducing that a walkthrough can judge, in particular UC02-F3-CL1 (introductions lead to repeat contact), UC02-F1-CL3 (accepting out of politeness) and UC02-F2-CL4 (matching feels exposing). Claims about outcomes over weeks (UC01-F3-CL2, UC02-F3-CL9) were out of reach and are left to ER-03.

Why then: the lab session was the course's slot for a formative walkthrough of each group's prototype, and Build 1 had just been deployed.
)))
|Method|(((
**What was done.** A group member ran the prototype on a laptop and two peers took turns as the resident, using the persona cards (Elena, Jan, Ria). They talked to the simulated Pepper through the browser, and gave feedback during and after the session. A second group member discussed the prototype with the lecturer in a separate call during the same session.

**What was recorded.** Nothing during the session. The facilitator was alone at the table and could not take notes while running the prototype. The findings below were reported by the two group members in the group chat on the same afternoon. Build 1 has a six-item feedback form (##logs/feedback.jsonl##); whether the peers filled it in is not known.

**Limits.** Two peers, one session, no notes. The peers are not people with dementia. The facilitator found the walkthrough procedure unclear on the day, so the four questions were not asked systematically. The session walked the walking version of the concept, not the narrowed UC01 to UC03, and the peers did not know that only the left side of the screen is what a resident would see (KF-04). The findings can say that a problem was noticed, not how often it would occur or how serious it would be for residents.
)))

